1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:05,400
To write a single token, Llama 3.1 8B
does a lot of arithmetic.

2
00:00:06,017 --> 00:00:09,017
About 15.5 billion operations.

3
00:00:09,617 --> 00:00:13,917
More than 99.9% of them are
one calculation.

4
00:00:14,217 --> 00:00:19,442
Multiply two lists of numbers, position by
position, and add up the results.

5
00:00:20,450 --> 00:00:23,050
A twelve-year-old can do that on paper.

6
00:00:23,650 --> 00:00:25,750
The hard part is bookkeeping.

7
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:31,250
Billions of numbers, in grids of different
sizes, and knowing which grid is which.

8
00:00:32,250 --> 00:00:37,235
By the end of this video, you'll be able to
read an einsum line from real model

9
00:00:37,235 --> 00:00:37,550
code.

10
00:00:38,150 --> 00:00:42,125
And say what shape it produces, and how
much arithmetic it costs.

11
00:00:45,150 --> 00:00:50,225
This is Part 1 of a 16-part series on the
math inside language models.

12
00:00:50,525 --> 00:00:53,675
If you remember high-school
algebra, that's enough.

13
00:00:54,683 --> 00:00:56,483
Start with a spreadsheet.

14
00:00:57,083 --> 00:00:59,483
One cell holds a single number.

15
00:00:59,783 --> 00:01:01,358
That's a scalar.

16
00:01:01,983 --> 00:01:05,408
A column is an ordered list of
numbers, a vector.

17
00:01:05,708 --> 00:01:10,383
Llama turns each token into a
vector of 4,096 numbers.

18
00:01:10,983 --> 00:01:14,308
A whole sheet, with rows and
columns, is a matrix.

19
00:01:14,608 --> 00:01:17,383
Almost every learned weight lives in one.

20
00:01:17,983 --> 00:01:22,258
And a workbook of identical sheets is
a tensor with three axes.

21
00:01:22,558 --> 00:01:27,083
In general, a tensor is a block of
numbers with any number of axes.

22
00:01:28,083 --> 00:01:31,258
The list of axis sizes is the
tensor's shape.

23
00:01:31,883 --> 00:01:40,260
Two sentences of five tokens, each token 4,096
numbers, make a tensor of shape 2, 5,

24
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:40,858
4,096.

25
00:01:41,483 --> 00:01:44,533
To count its numbers, multiply the sizes.

26
00:01:44,833 --> 00:01:47,558
That's 40,960.

27
00:01:48,583 --> 00:01:51,208
The analogy breaks in three places.

28
00:01:51,817 --> 00:01:57,736
A model's 4,096 numbers have no names, and
one rarely means anything you could

29
00:01:57,736 --> 00:01:58,192
label.

30
00:01:58,817 --> 00:02:02,492
The attention code inside Llama
juggles five axes.

31
00:02:03,117 --> 00:02:05,467
And nobody typed the numbers in.

32
00:02:05,767 --> 00:02:08,442
All 8 billion were learned in training.

33
00:02:09,450 --> 00:02:11,300
Now the one operation.

34
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:16,599
The dot product takes two lists of the same
length, multiplies them position by

35
00:02:16,599 --> 00:02:18,750
position, and adds up the results.

36
00:02:19,350 --> 00:02:21,575
One times seven is 7.

37
00:02:22,183 --> 00:02:24,258
Two times nine is 18.

38
00:02:24,883 --> 00:02:27,408
Three times eleven is 33.

39
00:02:28,017 --> 00:02:30,992
Add them up, and the dot product is 58.

40
00:02:31,292 --> 00:02:34,442
In a spreadsheet, that's the
SUMPRODUCT formula.

41
00:02:35,450 --> 00:02:39,750
A matrix multiply is that same
formula, filled across a grid.

42
00:02:40,050 --> 00:02:45,250
Take a 2 by 3 matrix, A, with rows
1, 2, 3 and 4, 5, 6.

43
00:02:45,850 --> 00:02:51,500
And a 3 by 2 matrix, B, with columns
7, 9, 11 and 8, 10, 12.

44
00:02:52,517 --> 00:02:57,542
Each entry of the answer is one dot product,
a row of A with a column of B.

45
00:02:58,150 --> 00:03:01,025
Row 1 with column 1 is our 58.

46
00:03:01,650 --> 00:03:04,525
Row 1 with column 2 gives 64.

47
00:03:05,150 --> 00:03:08,950
Row 2 with column 1 gives 139.

48
00:03:09,550 --> 00:03:13,375
And row 2 with column 2 gives 154.

49
00:03:13,675 --> 00:03:16,150
Four entries, four dot products.

50
00:03:17,150 --> 00:03:20,250
A dot product needs two lists
of the same length.

51
00:03:20,550 --> 00:03:25,250
So a row of the first matrix must be as
long as a column of the second.

52
00:03:25,550 --> 00:03:28,300
That gives the one rule worth memorizing.

53
00:03:28,917 --> 00:03:32,142
The inner sizes must match,
and they disappear.

54
00:03:32,442 --> 00:03:34,567
The outer sizes survive.

55
00:03:35,583 --> 00:03:36,908
Your turn.

56
00:03:37,208 --> 00:03:42,008
Our example was 2 by 3, times 3 by
2, which gave 2 by 2.

57
00:03:42,308 --> 00:03:43,883
Flip the order.

58
00:03:44,183 --> 00:03:45,658
What comes out?

59
00:03:50,683 --> 00:03:52,333
3 by 3.

60
00:03:52,633 --> 00:03:57,708
The inner sizes are both 2, so it works, but
the result is a different size.

61
00:03:58,008 --> 00:04:01,108
Order matters in matrix multiplication.

62
00:04:02,117 --> 00:04:04,467
The shape also tells you the cost.

63
00:04:04,767 --> 00:04:07,392
The result has m times n entries.

64
00:04:08,017 --> 00:04:13,242
Each is a dot product of length k, so k
multiplications and k additions.

65
00:04:13,850 --> 00:04:17,425
Each one is called a FLOP, a
floating-point operation.

66
00:04:17,725 --> 00:04:20,525
So about 2mkn FLOPs in all.

67
00:04:21,550 --> 00:04:23,025
A real one.

68
00:04:23,325 --> 00:04:33,224
Llama 3.1 8B's query projection multiplies each
token's 4,096 numbers by a 4,096 by

69
00:04:33,224 --> 00:04:34,775
4,096 matrix.

70
00:04:35,383 --> 00:04:39,308
For one token, that's 33.6 million FLOPs.

71
00:04:39,917 --> 00:04:46,867
For eight prompts of 1,000 tokens, stacked
into 8,000 rows, it's 268 billion.

72
00:04:47,483 --> 00:04:49,283
Only m changed.

73
00:04:49,583 --> 00:04:51,633
The weights didn't change at all.

74
00:04:51,933 --> 00:04:56,808
They're reused 8,000 times, which is
why GPUs like big batches.

75
00:04:57,817 --> 00:05:01,992
Now let's follow real data
through Llama 3.1 8B.

76
00:05:02,292 --> 00:05:06,917
Every shape in it comes from a handful of
numbers in its config file.

77
00:05:07,517 --> 00:05:11,642
Each token is a vector of 4,096 numbers.

78
00:05:12,250 --> 00:05:17,650
There are 32 blocks, each with 32
query heads of 128 numbers.

79
00:05:18,250 --> 00:05:22,975
Keys and values get only 8 heads, each
shared by 4 query heads.

80
00:05:23,275 --> 00:05:25,650
That's called grouped-query attention.

81
00:05:26,250 --> 00:05:31,675
The MLP inside each block is 14,336 wide.

82
00:05:31,975 --> 00:05:37,300
And the vocabulary has 128,256 tokens.

83
00:05:38,317 --> 00:05:40,467
Here's the whole trip, in shapes.

84
00:05:40,767 --> 00:05:45,917
B is the number of sequences in the batch, and
T is the number of tokens in each.

85
00:05:46,517 --> 00:05:50,342
Token IDs, B by T, go through
the embedding lookup.

86
00:05:50,642 --> 00:05:53,467
That's a copy, with zero arithmetic.

87
00:05:53,767 --> 00:05:57,942
Out comes the stream: B by T by 4,096.

88
00:05:58,550 --> 00:06:06,288
Inside each block, the query projection's
4,096 numbers are cut into 32 heads of

89
00:06:06,288 --> 00:06:06,675
128.

90
00:06:06,975 --> 00:06:09,650
That's a reshape, with no arithmetic.

91
00:06:09,950 --> 00:06:12,225
Keys and values get 8 heads.

92
00:06:12,850 --> 00:06:16,825
Then each head compares a token
with every visible token.

93
00:06:17,125 --> 00:06:21,875
That's a T by T grid of scores per head,
the only place where tokens meet.

94
00:06:22,483 --> 00:06:26,908
The values are mixed and
projected back to 4,096.

95
00:06:27,208 --> 00:06:32,758
Then the MLP widens each token
to 14,336 numbers.

96
00:06:33,383 --> 00:06:36,558
And brings it back down to 4,096.

97
00:06:36,858 --> 00:06:42,858
A block takes in B by T by 4,096, and
hands out exactly the same shape.

98
00:06:43,158 --> 00:06:46,633
That's why 32 identical
blocks can be stacked.

99
00:06:47,250 --> 00:06:54,705
After the last block, an output table turns each
token into 128,256 scores, one for

100
00:06:54,705 --> 00:06:57,400
every token in the vocabulary.

101
00:06:58,017 --> 00:07:03,467
The scores grid grows with T squared, and every
other tensor grows with B times T.

102
00:07:04,483 --> 00:07:07,933
Count the FLOPs of every step,
and one part dominates.

103
00:07:08,550 --> 00:07:14,425
The three MLP matrices do 75% of the
arithmetic spent on weights.

104
00:07:14,725 --> 00:07:20,100
The attention projections do 18%,
and the output table 7%.

105
00:07:21,117 --> 00:07:23,342
Here's a shortcut people use.

106
00:07:23,642 --> 00:07:28,617
Llama 3.1 8B has 8.03 billion parameters.

107
00:07:28,917 --> 00:07:32,542
How many FLOPs per token does the
rule of thumb predict?

108
00:07:37,550 --> 00:07:39,450
About 16 billion.

109
00:07:39,750 --> 00:07:44,275
Two FLOPs per parameter, one multiply
and one add for every weight.

110
00:07:45,283 --> 00:07:48,583
The shortcut gives 16.06 billion.

111
00:07:48,883 --> 00:07:51,733
Two corrections turn it into a real count.

112
00:07:52,350 --> 00:07:58,300
The embedding table holds 525 million
parameters, but a lookup is a copy.

113
00:07:58,600 --> 00:08:01,450
That removes 1.05 billion.

114
00:08:02,050 --> 00:08:09,348
Attention over 1,000 tokens of context adds 0.52
billion that no parameter accounts

115
00:08:09,348 --> 00:08:09,700
for.

116
00:08:10,317 --> 00:08:16,562
Counting every step of the forward pass gives
15.53 billion, within 4% of the

117
00:08:16,562 --> 00:08:17,292
shortcut.

118
00:08:18,317 --> 00:08:21,192
But the attention term grows with context.

119
00:08:21,817 --> 00:08:26,667
At 1,000 tokens, attention adds half a
billion FLOPs per token.

120
00:08:27,283 --> 00:08:33,933
At 8,192 tokens, it's 4.3
billion, 22% of the total.

121
00:08:34,550 --> 00:08:41,850
At 128,000 tokens, the per-token cost becomes
82 billion, five times the shortcut.

122
00:08:42,850 --> 00:08:46,925
So a forward pass always costs 2
FLOPs per parameter?

123
00:08:47,550 --> 00:08:49,625
Only for short contexts.

124
00:08:49,925 --> 00:08:53,325
At long context, attention
takes over the bill.

125
00:08:54,350 --> 00:08:56,375
Now the line from the start.

126
00:08:56,675 --> 00:09:01,150
Code needs a compact way to say which
axes get multiplied and summed.

127
00:09:01,450 --> 00:09:04,950
Einsum notation gives every axis a letter.

128
00:09:05,550 --> 00:09:08,250
B T D names the axes of x.

129
00:09:08,550 --> 00:09:12,275
Batch, token, and the 4,096 numbers.

130
00:09:12,883 --> 00:09:14,758
D F names the weight.

131
00:09:15,058 --> 00:09:19,833
4,096 in, 14,336 out.

132
00:09:20,450 --> 00:09:22,750
B T F names the result.

133
00:09:23,350 --> 00:09:27,200
The rule is that any letter missing
from the output gets summed.

134
00:09:27,500 --> 00:09:34,119
D is in both inputs but not the output, so
that's a dot product of length 4,096 for

135
00:09:34,119 --> 00:09:35,475
every b, t and f.

136
00:09:36,083 --> 00:09:42,083
The result is B by T by 14,336, read
straight from the string.

137
00:09:43,083 --> 00:09:47,108
Don't you need advanced math
to understand LLMs?

138
00:09:47,717 --> 00:09:48,942
No.

139
00:09:49,242 --> 00:09:54,572
A forward pass is multiplication and addition,
plus a few exponentials and square

140
00:09:54,572 --> 00:09:54,967
roots.

141
00:09:55,267 --> 00:09:59,917
Training adds derivatives, which Part 7
builds from the slope of a line.

142
00:10:00,517 --> 00:10:03,342
But tensors are something exotic.

143
00:10:03,950 --> 00:10:07,975
In machine learning, a tensor is a
block of numbers with a shape.

144
00:10:08,275 --> 00:10:13,000
A PyTorch tensor and a NumPy array are
both just numbers plus a shape.

145
00:10:13,617 --> 00:10:18,443
The functions that aren't matrix multiplies
are a tiny share of the work, but

146
00:10:18,443 --> 00:10:22,267
without them a stack of matrix
multiplies collapses into one.

147
00:10:22,567 --> 00:10:24,417
Part 3 shows why.

148
00:10:25,417 --> 00:10:30,567
Everything today used one toolkit, linear
algebra: what the model computes.

149
00:10:30,867 --> 00:10:35,892
It comes back later for attention, rotary
positions and low-rank compression.

150
00:10:36,517 --> 00:10:41,560
Probability and information cover what the
outputs mean, and how the training loss

151
00:10:41,560 --> 00:10:42,667
measures surprise.

152
00:10:43,283 --> 00:10:47,958
Calculus and optimization cover how 8
billion numbers get learned.

153
00:10:48,583 --> 00:10:53,658
And numerics and counting cover the cost,
from 4-bit numbers to scaling laws.

154
00:10:54,683 --> 00:10:56,283
So, to recap.

155
00:10:56,583 --> 00:11:02,051
A scalar is a number, a vector a list, a matrix
a grid, and a tensor a block with

156
00:11:02,051 --> 00:11:03,333
any number of axes.

157
00:11:03,950 --> 00:11:07,225
A matrix multiply is a grid
of dot products.

158
00:11:07,525 --> 00:11:12,550
Inner sizes match and vanish, and it
costs about 2mkn FLOPs.

159
00:11:13,150 --> 00:11:16,450
An LLM is dot products plus bookkeeping.

160
00:11:16,750 --> 00:11:19,900
Track the shapes, and you can
follow the whole model.

161
00:11:20,917 --> 00:11:26,979
Next is Part 2, a closer look at the dot
product: what length and angle mean, and

162
00:11:26,979 --> 00:11:33,117
why two random vectors in 4,096 dimensions are
almost always nearly perpendicular.

163
00:11:34,117 --> 00:11:38,815
The full article, with the complete shape
table and code you can run, is on

164
00:11:38,815 --> 00:11:39,442
vidyal.ai.

165
00:11:40,450 --> 00:11:42,150
Thanks for watching.

166
00:11:42,450 --> 00:11:45,700
Next in the series, vectors
and dot products.

167
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,075
Subscribe so you don't miss it.
